"""PLAN §5 P8 / §6.8 GPU admission, continuation and yield gate for the dltrain lane. Standard library only.

This module never imports torch and never initialises CUDA: the GPU is observed only through `nvidia-smi` CSV output.
It never opens or acquires the intake TabPFN lock: it stats the lock path and looks for that inode in `/proc/locks` text.
It never reads another process's `/proc/<pid>/cmdline` or `environ`; a GPU compute app is identified by the PID (and the
`process_name` column) that nvidia-smi reports, and any PID not declared as the trainer's own is FOREIGN.

Exact sources (any missing key, unparsable value or failed read refuses; nothing is defaulted):
- Q = `queue-status.json` (schema `jope.incoming-queue-status/v1`): `utc_ns_text` younger than 45 s and not in the future;
  `registry.states` is a sparse `GROUP BY state` count, so an absent state counts 0, but a missing `states` object or any
  key outside KNOWN_STATES refuses; `registry.poll.utc_ns` younger than 30 min and not in the future; `registry.items` is
  a list of objects whose `state` must be a known state.
- C = `cpu-admission.json`: `admit` must be `true`; `sampled_unix_seconds` at most 45 s old (and not more than 5 s in the
  future, as in cpuguard); `nodes.<host>.cpu_rolling_percent` (host = this host, `socket.gethostname()` by default).
- L = `tabpfn-gpu.lock`: held iff its `major:minor:inode` appears in `/proc/locks` (holders and blocked waiters both count).
- `systemctl --user list-units <unit_glob> --state=active,activating,deactivating,reloading --no-legend --plain`: any line
  is an intake GPU unit. The PLAN names `--state=active`; the extra states are a strict superset (fail safe), because the
  intake queue itself treats `activating` as alive and a stopping unit may still hold GPU memory.
- `nvidia-smi --query-gpu=...` and `--query-compute-apps=...` (CSV, no header, no units). Every reported GPU must pass.
- `os.statvfs(disk_path)` free bytes (f_bavail * f_frsize) against the DL-D7 host floor (300 GB).
Every probe records the sha256 of each file it read (Q, C, the /proc/locks snapshot) and of each command's stdout in the
verdict's `observations`, and parses exactly the bytes it hashed.

Owner decision DL-D14: "GPU admission and scoring block on queued, discovered, validated and benchmarking intake items and
on any active jopedime-incoming-gpu-* unit; benchmark_recovery_required undecided". Until the owner rules on it,
`benchmark_recovery_required` is treated as BLOCKING (fail safe), so BLOCKING_STATES has five members.

Codex `neural-encoding-queue-v1` GPU workers (and anything else not declared as ours) are foreign compute apps: REFUSE
before launch, YIELD after. The trainer never preempts them.

Decisions:
- `admission_probe()` -> ADMIT or REFUSE. REFUSE on any intake signal; any foreign compute app; free < 20480 MiB;
  utilization >= 5 %; temperature >= 80 C; C stale, missing or `admit` not true; host rolling CPU >= 75 %; disk below floor.
- `continuation_probe(self_pid, state=...)` -> CONTINUE, PAUSE, YIELD, STOP or ABORT_GATE (precedence STOP > ABORT_GATE >
  YIELD > PAUSE > CONTINUE; every reason is listed). Intake signal, foreign compute app or a failed/unparsable GPU query ->
  YIELD; C stale/missing, `admit` not true or rolling >= 75 -> PAUSE until rolling < 70 continuously for 60 s, and YIELD once
  paused for more than 600 s; temperature >= 80 C -> PAUSE (resumes when below 80), >= 85 C -> STOP; disk below floor ->
  ABORT_GATE (final checkpoint, `ABORTED_GATE`). The trainer's own PIDs are excluded from compute apps; free memory and
  utilisation are not continuation criteria because the trainer holds its own reserve.

Intake signals (`intake_signals()`, all fail closed): lock held or not checkable; an active intake GPU unit or a failed unit
query; a benchmarking item or a blocking-state item; a blocking state count > 0; an unknown state key or item state; Q or
its poll stale, in the future, missing, malformed or unreadable.

`Watchdog` probes at least every `interval_s` (<= 15 s), sets `yield_requested` on YIELD/STOP/ABORT_GATE, and, once any
intake signal has been seen (a probe exception counts as one, because intake can then no longer be excluded), calls
`hard_exit(75)` after `hard_exit_s` (<= 60 s) unless `disarm()` was called first, so the trainer releases its GPU reserve
only by exiting. A trainer that must record `YIELDED_UNPUBLISHED` passes a `hard_exit` that records it, then `os._exit`s.
`hard_exit_s` is measured from the first probe that saw the signal, so the bound from signal onset is at most
`interval_s + hard_exit_s` (plus one probe's duration); a launch that must hold the PLAN's 60 s-from-onset bound strictly
sets `hard_exit_s = 60 - interval_s`.
"""
import argparse
import hashlib
